{"id": "38b925da-fa52-49a1-b643-4047f57eda05", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "tool_summary", "content": [{"type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "tooluse_n5lyXUL3LUCS2f8Px5MbTw", "status": "success", "content": [{"text": "## Documentation search results

{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Build trustworthy AI agents with Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Observability | Artificial Intelligence","context":"## Prerequisites\n\nTo enable Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Observability for your AI agents, you must have the following prerequisites:\n\n* An active AWS account with model access enabled to the FM you want to use and credentials configured.\n* Transaction search enabled on CloudWatch. First-time users must enable CloudWatch transaction search to view Amazon Bedrock AgentCore spans and traces.\n* The OpenTelemetry library. To add it, include `aws-opentelemetry-distro` (ADOT) in your `requirements.txt` file.\n\nFor agents hosted on Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Runtime, observability is automatically enabled with no additional configurations. For agents not hosted on Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Runtime, you must create a log group and log stream for your agent in CloudWatch, which you can use to configure the following environment variables:\n\n* `AGENT_OBSERVABILITY_ENABLED=true` – Activates the ADOT pipeline\n* `OTEL_PYTHON_DISTRO=aws_distro` – Uses AWS Distro for OpenTelemetry\n* `OTEL_PYTHON_CONFIGURATOR=aws_configurator` – Sets the AWS configurator for ADOT SDK\n* `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_PROTOCOL=http/protobuf` – Configures the export protocol\n* `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_LOGS_HEADERS=x-aws-log-group=`<YOUR-LOG-GROUP>, `x-aws-log-stream=`<YOUR-LOG-STREAM>, and `x-aws-metric-namespace=`<YOUR-NAMESPACE> – Directs logs to CloudWatch groups\n* `OTEL_RESOURCE_ATTRIBUTES=service.name=<YOUR-AGENT-NAME>` – Identifies your agent in observability data\n\nYou can manage these environment variables using an .env file to keep AWS credentials separate from your code. For an example template, refer to the following env.example file","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/build-trustworthy-ai-agents-with-amazon-bedrock-agentcore-observability/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Debugging production agents with Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Observability | Artificial Intelligence","context":"### Enabling observability\n\nBefore you begin debugging, enable CloudWatch Transaction Search for your account. This allows Amazon Bedrock AgentCore to send trace and metric data to CloudWatch. After you enable this setting, the service begins collecting observability data across your agents, memory systems, and tool integrations. You can then access this data through CloudWatch dashboards and Logs Insights queries","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/debugging-production-agents-with-amazon-bedrock-agentcore-observability/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"Domain 2: Operational reliability","context":"## Trace requests across multi-agent workflows\n\nMulti-agent workflows hand requests between agents that delegate tasks and pass context. Without correlation, you can't tell which agent started a workflow, what context the agent passed, or where a chain of decisions went wrong.\n\n### Add correlation metadata to every agent\n\nTreat agent instrumentation like any production-distributed system. Include correlation metadata (agent_id, session_id, and trace_id) in structured logs. Propagate trace context through every inter-agent message. Watch for cascading decisions, where one agent's action triggers chains of downstream decisions that amplify errors.\n\n### Use AWS X-Ray and CloudWatch Transaction Search\n\n[AWS X-Ray](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/xray/latest/devguide/xray-services-agentcore.html) provides distributed tracing for multi-agent systems. It uses the X-Amzn-Trace-Id header to propagate trace context across service boundaries and create a causal chain that links operations across agents. For agents on AgentCore Runtime, turn on CloudWatch Transaction Search once. Then, AgentCore Observability emits OpenTelemetry-compatible traces to CloudWatch automatically. You can view these traces through the Generative AI Observability dashboard or CloudWatch Transaction Search.\n\nFor agents implemented as Lambda functions, Amazon Elastic Container Service (Amazon ECS) tasks, or other compute services, instrument them with AWS Distro for OpenTelemetry (ADOT) to generate AWS X-Ray traces. Propagate the X-Amzn-Trace-Id header through all inter-agent messages: EventBridge events, Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) messages, Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) notifications, and API calls. Include correlation metadata in structured logs so that logs and traces tie together.\n\n### Correlate AWS DevOps Agent investigations with downstream MCP servers\n\nAWS DevOps Agent investigations that reach external systems through connected MCP servers create a tracing consideration. AWS DevOps Agent publishes vended logs and CloudWatch metrics for investigation activity, and the AWS DevOps Agent Journal captures MCP server calls. AWS DevOps Agent manages internal orchestration of MCP tool calls so you don't have to.\n\nOn the MCP server side, where you do have control, emit traces and structured logs so that each downstream call from the agent can have end-to-end correlation. Skills that direct AWS DevOps Agent to use specific MCP tools can document expected logging behavior so that you can trace investigation activity from the Agent Space to the resources that those tools touch","url":"https://repost.aws/articles/ARRrnN9FMVRve7wI2svOTiRg/domain-2-operational-reliability"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"Get started with AgentCore Observability","context":"## Prerequisites\n\nBefore starting, make sure you have:\n\n* **AWS Account** with credentials configured ( `aws configure` ) with model access enabled to the Foundation Model you would like to use.\n* **Python 3.10+** installed\n* **Enable transaction search** on Amazon CloudWatch. Only once, first-time users must enable CloudWatch Transaction Search to view Amazon Bedrock AgentCore spans and traces. If you deploy with the AgentCore CLI, `agentcore deploy` enables Transaction Search for you.\n* **(Non-runtime agents only) Add the OpenTelemetry library** – Include `aws-opentelemetry-distro` (ADOT) in your requirements.txt file. If you host your agent on AWS Lambda, use the AWS Lambda Layer for OpenTelemetry on the AWS Distro for OpenTelemetry website instead.\n* **(Non-runtime agents only)** Make sure that your framework is configured to emit traces (for example, `strands-agents[otel]` package). You may sometimes need to include your agent framework’s auto-instrumentor (for example, `opentelemetry-instrumentation-langchain` ).\n\nAmazon Bedrock AgentCore Observability offers two ways to configure monitoring to match different infrastructure needs:\n\n1. Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Runtime-hosted agents\n2. Non-runtime hosted agents\n\nAs a one time setup per AWS account, first time users need to enable Transaction Search on Amazon CloudWatch. There are two ways to do this, via the API and via the CloudWatch Console. If you deploy your agent with the AgentCore CLI, `agentcore deploy` enables Transaction Search automatically and you can skip Step 1. To opt out, run `agentcore config transactionSearch false`","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock-agentcore/latest/devguide/observability-get-started.html"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"Build context-rich research agents with Deep Agents and Bedrock AgentCore | Artificial Intelligence","context":"## Trace and debug your agent\n\nAgentCore Observability gives you visibility into this multi-agent architecture through Amazon CloudWatch. AgentCore emits traces and spans in OpenTelemetry (OTEL) format, so you can view the full orchestration hierarchy on the CloudWatch GenAI Observability page: the coordinator’s run at the top level, a child span for each research subagent, and the analyst subagent that follows. Within each span, you can review tool calls with their inputs, outputs, timing, and token usage, confirm that the research subagents ran concurrently from their wall-clock timing, and identify which subagent and tool call encountered an issue when a browser navigation or code run does not succeed. As a one-time setup per account, you enable CloudWatch Transaction Search before traces and spans become available. When you host the agent on AgentCore Runtime (Part 2), the runtime instruments your agent with OTEL automatically, so no additional configuration is needed. To run the same agent outside the runtime, add the AWS Distro for OpenTelemetry (ADOT) SDK and the LangChain instrumentation library to your agent. For more information, refer to the Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Observability documentation.\n\nYou can also score the quality of these traces with Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Evaluations, which provides built-in evaluators such as goal success rate and tool selection accuracy. For more details, refer to the AgentCore Evaluations documentation.\n\nIf you prefer, you can also use LangSmith for tracing. With LangSmith, you get end-to-end tracing that helps you debug this multi-agent architecture. Set three environment variables to turn on automatic tracing:\n\n```\nexport LANGCHAIN_TRACING_V2=true\nexport LANGCHAIN_API_KEY=\"your-langsmith-api-key\"\nexport LANGCHAIN_PROJECT=\"competitive-research-agent\"\n```\n\nThe accompanying notebook reviews this option","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/build-context-rich-research-agents-with-deep-agents-and-bedrock-agentcore/"}]}}

